# buddi configuration.
#
# `buddi init` copies this file to `.env` and fills in what it asks you about.
# Everything here is optional unless it says otherwise; a commented line shows
# the default that is already in effect, so uncommenting one changes nothing
# until you change its value.
#
# Two variables are deliberately NOT settable here — they are generated and
# kept in the vault, and the notes below say where: BUDDI_DB_PASSWORD and
# BUDDI_WEB_TOKEN.
#
# This file is gitignored and written at mode 600. `buddi vault import-env`
# moves every secret in it into the OS keychain and rewrites each line to
# NAME="<vault>", which buddi reads as "ask the vault". The quotes are
# deliberate: unquoted, `<` is a shell redirection and `. ./.env` would fail —
# dotenv strips them, so both spellings mean the same to buddi.


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Required: the model credential
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Set ONE of these two. With a Claude subscription run `claude setup-token` and
# paste the result into CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN; otherwise use an API key from
# console.anthropic.com. Nothing is discovered — an agent names the variable it
# wants, and resolution fails closed when it is missing or empty. With neither
# set, nothing runs at all.
CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Second provider (optional). Only agents whose file pins `provider: openai`
# read this — @scout ships that way. Leave it empty and @scout is listed as
# unavailable; every other agent is unaffected. There is no subscription-token
# form here and no fallback to the Anthropic key.
# OPENAI_API_KEY=

# Optional account-level Claude OAuth login (experimental). Apply migration 020.
# Tokens are saved in the host vault, not here. See docs/anthropic-oauth.md.
# BUDDI_ANTHROPIC_OAUTH_EXPERIMENT=1

# The model an agent gets when its own file pins none. Each is that provider's
# default and is never handed to the other one. Per-agent:
# `buddi agents set <handle> --model`.
# BUDDI_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5
# BUDDI_OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-5


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The vault
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Where secrets live. macOS defaults to the OS keychain; every other platform
# defaults to an encrypted file, because there is no keychain to use.
#   keychain  the OS keychain (macOS)
#   file      an AES-256-GCM file, and BUDDI_VAULT_KEY below is the key
#   memory    this process only — for tests
#   none      no vault at all: every secret comes from this file, as on day 1
# BUDDI_VAULT=keychain

# REQUIRED BY THE FILE VAULT. It *is* the key, and it is deliberately not stored
# beside the file it unlocks. `buddi init` generates one here if there is none;
# it is the only command that ever mints one, so two processes can never end up
# with two different keys over one vault.
#
# Losing this line loses every secret in the vault, including the database
# password — which makes the database unreachable with all the data still
# intact. A backup never contains it, on purpose. Keep a copy off this machine.
# BUDDI_VAULT_KEY=

# Where the file vault's bytes are. Default $BUDDI_HOME/vault.json.
# BUDDI_VAULT_FILE=

# buddi's own home directory: the parent of the file vault, and the fallback
# home for your agents and skills when there is no `private/` in the repo.
# BUDDI_HOME=~/.buddi


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The database
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# There is deliberately no password here. buddi generates one at `buddi init`
# (or on the first `buddi db up`), keeps it in the vault as BUDDI_DB_PASSWORD,
# and assembles DATABASE_URL around it at runtime — so no file on this machine
# holds the credential to your financial history, your mail and your
# conversations. `buddi db secure` migrates an installation that predates that.
#
# The escape hatch: set DATABASE_URL here and it wins over everything. That is
# for running your own Postgres; buddi will then neither generate nor rotate a
# password, because it did not issue that one.
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:password@host:5432/buddi

# Host port for the postgres container. Set it alone — the assembled
# DATABASE_URL reads it. Only set the port in DATABASE_URL too if you are also
# setting DATABASE_URL by hand.
#
# The container publishes on 127.0.0.1 only (see docker-compose.yml). The one
# legitimate reason to change that is reaching this database from another
# machine of your own over a *private* network — a Tailscale or WireGuard
# address. Never bind it to 0.0.0.0 on a network you do not control.
# BUDDI_DB_PORT=5432


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where your things live
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Your agents and skills. The examples this repo ships are loaded first and
# these override them, by id and by name. Unset, buddi uses `private/agents`
# and `private/skills` at the repo root if they exist, else `~/.buddi/agents`
# and `~/.buddi/skills`. `buddi init` writes BUDDI_AGENTS_DIR here if you pick
# somewhere else; `buddi doctor`'s `config` row prints what resolved.
# BUDDI_AGENTS_DIR=
# BUDDI_SKILLS_DIR=

# Everything the installation writes: artifacts (the files you hand an agent),
# logs, backups, the terminal's history, and the dashboard token where there is
# no vault. Default `data/` at the repo root. Gitignored.
# BUDDI_DATA_DIR=

# The record of which plugins are installed. Default `plugins.json` next to
# your agents. Never committed.
# BUDDI_PLUGINS_FILE=

# The owner's timezone (IANA name). It is the day "today" means to every agent
# and tool, and the zone scheduled missions fire in.
# BUDDI_TZ=America/New_York

# What the agents call you. `buddi init` asks for it and records it here — but
# the name the agents actually use comes from the owner profile in the
# database, which the first conversation fills in. This line is a note to
# yourself, not a setting anything reads at runtime.
# BUDDI_OWNER_NAME=


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Web search (the `web` plugin)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Which backend `web.search` uses. This decides which company sees the
# questions your agents ask, so it is configuration and not a default anyone
# guesses at.
#   (unset)  Anthropic agents search through Anthropic itself, on the
#            credential the run already pays for; everything else uses Tavily
#   native   every agent searches through its own provider. An agent on a
#            provider with no server-side search can then read a page but not
#            search
#   tavily   always Tavily, needs TAVILY_API_KEY
#   brave    always Brave, needs BRAVE_SEARCH_API_KEY
# A name this build does not have is not a silent fallback: `buddi doctor`
# shows the typo.
# BUDDI_SEARCH_PROVIDER=

# The key for whichever backend you chose. Both are secrets and both move into
# the vault with `buddi vault import-env`. With neither set and no provider-side
# search, `web.search` returns "not configured" rather than an error, and says
# so to the agent so it cannot pretend it looked something up. `web.read` needs
# no key at all.
# TAVILY_API_KEY=            # free key: https://app.tavily.com
# BRAVE_SEARCH_API_KEY=      # https://brave.com/search/api/

# How many provider-side searches one turn may spend. A cost knob; values above
# 10 are clamped to 10.
# BUDDI_WEB_SEARCH_MAX_USES=3


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Telegram (optional)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Ask @BotFather for a bot token, then pair a device with `buddi telegram pair`.
# Without a token the Telegram surface is simply off; the terminal and the
# dashboard are unaffected.
# TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=

# The other way in, for a first boot before anything is paired: your numeric
# Telegram user id, which makes you the owner without a pairing code. Unset,
# every message from every sender is ignored until a device pairs — which is
# the intended state, so leave these empty unless you know you want them.
# `/id` in the chat prints both numbers.
# TELEGRAM_OWNER_USER_ID=
# TELEGRAM_OWNER_CHAT_ID=    # defaults to the user id


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Email (the `email` plugin, optional)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# One Gmail account, app password for v1 — Gmail OAuth refresh tokens for an
# unpublished app expire weekly, which is unusable for an unattended system. An
# app password is a full-mailbox bearer secret: it is not available under
# Advanced Protection and is revoked by a password change. With GMAIL_USER unset
# the plugin is installed but has nothing to poll, which is a valid running
# state.
# GMAIL_USER=you@gmail.com
# GMAIL_APP_PASSWORD=

# How long one poll of the inbox may take before it is abandoned. The source
# runner gives a poll twice this before it aborts it outright.
# EMAIL_POLL_TIMEOUT_MS=45000

# How many of the newest messages the very first poll pulls in. 0 means "start
# at now" — nothing that arrived before buddi did is read. Raise it once, on a
# first run, if you want some history triaged.
# EMAIL_BACKFILL=0


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Reminders (optional)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The budget an agent's own `reminder.set` is held to. Enforced in code, not in
# a prompt; the tool's description is built from these numbers, so the agent is
# told the limits this installation actually has. Out-of-range values are
# clamped, unparseable ones ignored — either way the default is used and the
# reason is logged once at startup.
# BUDDI_REMINDER_MIN_LEAD_MINUTES=5        # how far out a reminder must be (1..1440)
# BUDDI_REMINDER_MAX_HORIZON_DAYS=365      # how far out a reminder may be (1..3650)
# BUDDI_REMINDER_MAX_PENDING_PER_AGENT=10  # pending reminders one agent may hold (1..100)
# BUDDI_REMINDER_MAX_PENDING_TOTAL=25      # pending reminders across every agent (1..500)


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The local dashboard (optional)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Served by `buddi serve`; open it with `buddi dashboard`. The session token is
# generated on first run and kept in the vault under BUDDI_WEB_TOKEN (or
# <data dir>/web-token, mode 600, where there is no vault) — you never need to
# set it here.
# BUDDI_WEB=1                # 0 turns the dashboard off entirely
# BUDDI_WEB_HOST=127.0.0.1   # anything but loopback exposes an approval button
# BUDDI_WEB_PORT=4317

# Packaging escape hatches. The built UI, and the file the token falls back to
# where there is no vault. Defaults are `packages/web/dist` and
# `<data dir>/web-token`; you would only set these to serve a UI built
# somewhere else.
# BUDDI_WEB_ASSETS=
# BUDDI_WEB_TOKEN_FILE=


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Not for an owner
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# These are read by the code but are not configuration for a running
# installation, and are listed here only so that finding one in the source is
# not a mystery:
#
#   NO_COLOR / FORCE_COLOR   the usual terminal conventions: NO_COLOR set to
#                            anything turns ANSI off, FORCE_COLOR turns it on
#                            through a pipe. Not buddi's, and honoured as the
#                            standards define them.
#   XDG_CONFIG_HOME          where the Linux systemd user units are written.
#                            Standard; default ~/.config
#   BUDDI_TEST_AMBIENT       set and deleted inside one unit test, to prove that
#                            credentials are never discovered ambiently. Setting
#                            it does nothing.
#   PGPASSWORD               written into the environment of `psql` and
#                            `pg_dump` child processes. Never read from yours.
